n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-9I & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\end{bmatrix}-9\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-9I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 3\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$ \n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186371347 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371347 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371347 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371347 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371347 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371347 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371347 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371347 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371347 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b963a5db-95c9-59eb-9b48-d6d0b9964aae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371348 #edb/ref 1080 "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin